{{also|Stringer}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{suffix|en|string|er|t2=agent}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/stɹɪŋə/}}
* {{a|US|Canada}} {{IPA|en|/stɹɪŋɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-stringer.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪŋə(ɹ)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone who [[thread]]s something; one who makes or provides strings, especially for [[bow]]s.
#* '''1545''', {{w|Roger Ascham}}, ''Toxophilus''
#*: Be content to put your trust in honest '''stringers'''.
# Someone who [[string along|strings someone along]].
# A [[horizontal]] [[timber]] that [[support]]s [[upright]] [[post]]s, or supports the [[hull]] of a [[vessel]].
# The side rail supporting the rungs of a [[ladder]] or the steps of a [[flight]] of [[stair]]s.
# A small screw-hook to which [[piano]] strings are sometimes attached.
# {{lb|en|journalism}} A [[freelance]] [[correspondent]] not on the regular [[newspaper]] [[staff]], especially one retained on a [[part-time]] basis to report on events in a particular place.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|chapter=Enter Hyperspace|title=Generation X|author=Douglas Coupland|authorlink=Douglas Coupland|passage=And he told a few stories about time he had spent in New York in the 1950s as a '''stringer''' for the ''Asahi'' newspapers… about meeting Diana Vreeland and Truman Capote and Judy Holiday.}}
# {{lb|en|sports}} A person who plays on a particular [[string]].
# {{lb|en|surfing}} Wooden strip running lengthwise down the centre of a [[surfboard]], for strength.
# {{lb|en|baseball|slang|1800s}} A hard-hit ball.
# {{lb|en|fishing}} A [[cord]] or [[chain]], sometimes with additional [[loop|loops]], that is threaded through the mouth and [[gills]] of caught fish.
#* '''1970''', ''Field & Stream'' (volume 75, number 7, page 76)
#*: "Okay, that's a keeper," Harold said as he netted the 3-pounder and put him on a '''stringer''' over the side of the boat.
# A [[pallet]] or [[skid]] used when shipping [[less than truckload]] ([[LTL]]) freight. A platform typically constructed of timber or plastic designed such that freight may be stacked on top, able to be lifted by a [[forklift]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[libertine]]; a [[wencher]].
#* {{RQ:BFCT|The Knight of the Burning Pestle}}
#*: A whoreson tyrant! He has been an old '''stringer''' in's days
# {{lb|en|birdwatching}} A person who deliberately states that a certain bird is present when it is not; one who knowingly misleads other birders about the occurrence of a bird, especially a rarity.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1980|author=Bill Oddie|title=Bill Oddie's Little Black Bird Book|page=82|passage=[T]hose fellows know how to spot a '''stringer''' at work.}}

